# Pandas DataFrame Schema (tables/)

This document gives a schematic description of the two relational tables generated by the script `script/build_dataframes.py` and saved in **CSV** and **Parquet** formats inside the `tables/` folder.

---

## Structure and Relationship

- **Table 1 (`tabella_runs`)**: 1 row for each test/inference run performed by the LLM. Primary key: `run_id`.
- **Table 2 (`tabella_errori`)**: 1 row for each individual error detected by the error catalog. Foreign key: `run_id` (1-to-N relationship).

```
┌───────────────────────────────────────┐
│     Table 1: tabella_runs             │
├───────────────────────────────────────┤
│ PK: run_id                            │◀──┐
│ model_name, model_size, technique     │   │
│ context_id, prompt_type               │   │ (1 to N)
│ recall, precision, f1_score ...       │   │
│ dips_outcome, dips_error ...          │   │
│ total_errors, high_severity_errors... │   │
└───────────────────────────────────────┘   │
                                            │
┌───────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│     Table 2: tabella_errori           │   │
├───────────────────────────────────────┤   │
│ FK: run_id ───────────────────────────┼───┘
│ error_type                            │
│ error_subtype                         │
│ error_severity                        │
└───────────────────────────────────────┘
```

---

## Table 1: `tabella_runs` (`df_runs`)
**File**: `tables/tabella_runs.csv` | `tables/tabella_runs.parquet`  
**Granularity**: 1 row per single LLM run on a network intent.

| # | Column | Type | Source | Description / Examples |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | `run_id` | `string` | Computed | Unique ID: `{model_name}_{model_size}_{technique}_ctx{context_id}_intent{prompt_type}` (e.g. `Qwen2.5_7b_3shot_ctx34_intentC`) |
| 2 | `model_name` | `string` | Path | LLM family: `Qwen2.5` or `Gemini` |
| 3 | `model_size` | `string` | Path | Model size: `3b`, `7b`, `14b`, `3.5-flash` |
| 4 | `technique` | `string` | Path | Technique used: `0shot`, `3shot`, `9shot`, `LoRA` |
| 5 | `context_id` | `int64` | File name | Network context number (e.g. `context34` → `34`) |
| 6 | `prompt_type` | `string` | File name | Intent/prompt type: `A`, `B`, `C` |
| 7 | `recall` | `float64` | LLM report | Recall score in matching the generated Prolog facts (`0.0` - `1.0`) |
| 8 | `precision` | `float64` | LLM report | Precision score in matching the generated Prolog facts (`0.0` - `1.0`) |
| 9 | `jaccard` | `float64` | LLM report | Jaccard similarity index on the facts (`0.0` - `1.0`) |
| 10 | `f1_score` | `float64` | LLM report | Harmonic F1-Score between Precision and Recall (`0.0` - `1.0`) |
| 11 | `exact_match` | `int64` | Computed | `1` if `recall == 1.0` and `precision == 1.0`, otherwise `0` |
| 12 | `execution_time_s` | `float64` | Report / JSON | LLM inference time in seconds |
| 13 | `dips_outcome` | `string` | `report_dips.txt` | DIPS execution outcome: `SOLUTION`, `PROLOG_ERROR`, `TIMEOUT`, `FALSE`, `INTENT_ERROR`, `SWIPL_ERROR` |
| 14 | `dips_error` | `boolean` | DIPS file / Outcome | `True` if the DIPS execution failed with an error (syntax, timeout, crash), `False` otherwise, `<NA>` if missing |
| 15 | `jaccard_dips` | `float64` | DIPS file | Jaccard index computed on the DIPS placement (`JACCARD_DIPS:X.X`) |
| 16 | `intent` | `string` | LLM report | Natural-language text of the intent (enriched version with `<vnf, hw>` tags) |
| 17 | `generated_output` | `string` | LLM output file | Full Prolog text generated by the LLM (`output_contextN_intentX.txt`) |
| 18 | `gold_output` | `string` | `facts.pl` | Ground truth of the Prolog facts (`intent/contextN/facts.pl`) |
| 19 | `dips_execution` | `string` | DIPS file | Full textual output of the DIPS execution |
| 20 | `total_errors` | `int64` | Error catalog | Total number of errors catalogued for this run (`0` if no errors) |
| 21 | `high_severity_errors` | `int64` | Error catalog | Count of high-severity errors (`High` - they alter the semantics of the intent) |
| 22 | `low_severity_errors` | `int64` | Error catalog | Count of low-severity errors (`Low` - fixable in post-processing) |

